    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new `OwnerCreatedAuditEvent` record is genuinely well-conceived \u2014 an immutable event type is the right unit, co-locating serialization with the event is sensible, and the wiring into `addOwner` is minimal and additive. A few structural/idiomatic issues keep it from being clean, though:\n\n**1. Hand-rolled JSON serialization reinvents the codebase's serialization mechanism.** `toJson()` hand-builds the object and includes a full `jsonString` escaper (quotes, backslash, `\\n`/`\\r`/`\\t`, `\\uXXXX` control chars) and a `jsonNumber` null-handler \u2014 ~40 lines duplicating what Jackson already does. This is a Spring Boot app where Jackson is on the classpath and is what serializes every DTO in this very package; a maintainer here would expect an `ObjectMapper`/annotated record, not a bespoke escaper they now have to keep correct. If deterministic key order is the motivation, that's achievable with Jackson (`@JsonPropertyOrder`) without owning the escaping code. Recommend serializing via the framework's Jackson rather than a custom string builder.\n\n**2. Fully-qualified `AtomicLong` inline breaks the file's import convention.** Line 70 uses `java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong` inline (both in the field type and the `new`), and the Javadoc references it fully-qualified too. Every other type in this file is imported (lines 19\u201356). Add a normal `import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;` and use the short name for consistency.\n\n**3. `ownerMapper.membershipLevel(owner)` is computed twice.** It's called at line 146 (existing audit line) and again at line 148 (new event). Extract a single local (e.g. `Integer membershipLevel = ownerMapper.membershipLevel(owner);`) and reuse it in both spots to avoid the duplicated call.\n\nNone of these change what the code does; they align it with the surrounding Spring/Jackson idioms and the file's own conventions.",
